wn indexing and line numbering in `writeColumns`. The `colIdx` function returns an index into the lines slice, and the actual line number is `lineNum + idx`. For page 1 with lineNum=1, a line at index 0 gets number 1, and at index 55 gets number 56. For page 2, lineNum becomes 57, so the line at index 0 is correctly numbered 57. Now I need to verify this holds for multi-column layouts... I've verified the indexing logic is working correctly for both fill directions, so now I'm writing out the complete Go file with all the necessary imports and package setup.\n\nWriting the rest of the code... Parsing command-line arguments and setting up default options for page length, width, and number formatting, then iterating through the arguments to handle flags like `--length` and `--header` while collecting remaining files. Parsing more command-line flags... Writing flag parsing functions... Writing parser functions... Writing pagination logic... Writing more helper functions... Writing the page footer and body output... Writing column layout logic... Writing utility functions... Finishing the padding logic, then reconsidering whether content lines should have indentation applied while blank padding lines remain unindented—I think it's correct since trailing whitespace on empty lines is invisible anyway, though I'm second-guessing whether this matches GNU pr's behavior.\n\nI'm settling on not indenting blank lines for now and will adjust if the tests fail. The same logic applies to blank lines in the header, footer, and double-space mode—they stay unindented while actual content gets the indent applied.\n\nChecking the regression: when `opts.indent` is 0 (the default), the indent string is empty, so `writeBody` produces identical output to the original code. And with all the new options disabled, the behavior matches exactly.\n\nFor `writeColumns`, I need to verify the `fmtCell` function hasn't changed the logic. Comparing the original indexing...\n\nVerifying the cell forma